From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 15:05:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] netbpfload: skip uprobestatsbpfload on a kernel that cannot
 run it

netbpfload does not set bpf.progs_loaded itself. It execs
uprobestatsbpfload, which execs the platform bpfloader, which re-execs
netbpfload with "done", and only that last step sets the property.

On 4.9 the chain dies at the first link: uprobestatsbpfload takes SIGABRT
within 150 ms ("Service 'bpfloader' (pid 1581) received SIGABRT waiting
took 0.149000 seconds"), and init cannot even parse its .rc --
"capability 'PERFMON' not supported by the kernel", CAP_PERFMON being
5.8+.

bpf.progs_loaded then never appears, and bpf::waitForProgsLoaded() loops
forever. Two callers matter. netd is one. The other is
HealthLoop::UeventInit(), which calls it before attaching the uevent BPF
filter: the health HAL registers with servicemanager in Init(), then
blocks in UeventInit() and never reaches MainLoop(), so it never polls
binder. BatteryService.onStart() calls IHealth.registerCallback() on
system_server's main thread, that transaction is never serviced, and the
watchdog kills system_server at 66s -- a boot loop whose only symptom is
"Blocked in handler on main thread".

Skip only the link that cannot run and fall through to the platform
bpfloader, which has no hard kernel floor of its own and skips what it
cannot use. That keeps both halves of what the chain was for: the
platform programs under /system/etc/bpf get loaded -- gpuMem among them,
without which libmeminfo's BpfMapRO::abortOnMismatch() aborts
system_server from MemInfoReader.getGpuKernelUsedSizeKb() -- and the
platform loader re-execs us with "done" to set the property.
---
 bpf/loader/NetBpfLoad.cpp | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bpf/loader/NetBpfLoad.cpp b/bpf/loader/NetBpfLoad.cpp
index ddfff7b97f..af9ce76875 100644
--- a/bpf/loader/NetBpfLoad.cpp
+++ b/bpf/loader/NetBpfLoad.cpp
@@ -1874,8 +1874,20 @@ static int doLoad(char** argv, char * const envp[]) {
         return 0;
     }
 
+    // lineage-24.0: skip uprobestatsbpfload on a kernel that cannot run it, but keep the rest of
+    // the chain. It SIGABRTs within 150 ms here ("Service 'bpfloader' (pid 1581) received SIGABRT
+    // waiting took 0.149000 seconds") and init cannot even parse its .rc -- "capability 'PERFMON'
+    // not supported by the kernel", CAP_PERFMON being 5.8+. Losing the chain matters because its
+    // last link, netbpfload "done", is the only thing that sets bpf.progs_loaded, and every
+    // bpf::waitForProgsLoaded() caller then blocks forever: netd, and HealthLoop::UeventInit(),
+    // which wedges the health HAL before it polls binder, so BatteryService hangs in
+    // registerCallback and the watchdog kills system_server at 66s.
+    //
+    // Falling through to the platform bpfloader keeps both halves: it loads /system/etc/bpf (gpuMem
+    // among them, which libmeminfo's BpfMapRO aborts system_server without) and re-execs us with
+    // "done". It has no hard kernel floor of its own; like this loader it skips what it cannot use.
     // unreachable before U QPR3
-    if (exists(uprobestatsBpfLoader)) {
+    if (exists(uprobestatsBpfLoader) && isAtLeastKernelVersion(5, 8)) {
       ALOGI("done, transferring control to uprobestatsbpfload.");
       const char *args[] = {
           uprobestatsBpfLoader,
